From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:40:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: check the MDIO timeout before the
 last read

mtk_mdio_busy_wait() reads PHY_IAC_ACCESS and then checks the time. If
the thread is held up between the two for longer than what is left of
the timeout (by interrupts, or by preemption on a preemptible kernel),
it reports a timeout for an access that finished in the meantime.

Look at the clock before each read instead, so the last read is always
made after the timeout has passed.

[Based on neal.yen's (MediaTek) "fix spurious MDIO timeout", pesa1234's
 999-9921, which uses read_poll_timeout() and so sleeps 3-10 us between
 reads. This keeps the busy loop, which suits an MDIO access of a few
 tens of microseconds. On MilkFlint's kernel (PREEMPT_NONE) the window
 takes a second of interrupts, so this is for correctness only.]

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
index 2d179f7..3ec4cd5 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -350,9 +350,15 @@ static int mtk_mdio_busy_wait(struct mtk_eth *eth)
 	unsigned long t_start = jiffies;
 
 	while (1) {
+		/* Look at the clock before reading, so the last read is made
+		 * after the timeout and an access that finished while this
+		 * thread was held up isn't reported as timed out.
+		 */
+		bool timed_out = time_after(jiffies, t_start + PHY_IAC_TIMEOUT);
+
 		if (!(mtk_r32(eth, MTK_PHY_IAC) & PHY_IAC_ACCESS))
 			return 0;
-		if (time_after(jiffies, t_start + PHY_IAC_TIMEOUT))
+		if (timed_out)
 			break;
 		cond_resched();
 	}
